WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.866
The ocean absorbs a large share of humanity's
carbon emissions,

00:00:04.946 --> 00:00:10.865
yet our official picture of how much it takes up
always lags reality by about a year.

00:00:10.945 --> 00:00:16.582
This work introduces Carbon Monitor Ocean, or
CMO-NRT, a near-real-time, monthly,

00:00:16.662 --> 00:00:22.017
gridded dataset of global surface ocean fugacity of
CO2 and air-sea CO2 flux.

00:00:22.097 --> 00:00:28.439
By using convolutional neural networks and
semi-supervised learning to update the estimates

00:00:28.519 --> 00:00:32.745
from ten ocean biogeochemical models and eight data
products,

00:00:32.825 --> 00:00:37.332
the authors extend the global carbon budget to the
present month,

00:00:37.412 --> 00:00:43.190
giving scientists and policymakers a far more
timely view of the ocean carbon sink.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:47.776
The ocean is one of the largest buffers against
climate change,

00:00:47.856 --> 00:00:51.585
soaking up a big fraction of the carbon dioxide we emit.

00:00:51.665 --> 00:00:55.394
But our best official accounting of how much it absorbs,

00:00:55.474 --> 00:01:00.224
the annual Global Carbon Budget, always trails
reality by about a year.

00:01:00.304 --> 00:01:03.081
That means when the ocean's uptake shifts,

00:01:03.161 --> 00:01:06.482
we simply cannot see it until long after the fact.

00:01:06.562 --> 00:01:11.516
This lag is baked in: the biogeochemical models are
computationally heavy,

00:01:11.596 --> 00:01:17.570
and the surface-ocean observations they rely on are
themselves delayed by roughly a year.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:22.435
As countries move through the global stocktake
under the Paris Agreement,

00:01:22.515 --> 00:01:26.813
they need to know where the carbon is going right
now, not a year ago.

00:01:26.893 --> 00:01:31.504
The existing databases of ocean carbon fluxes are
detailed and trustworthy,

00:01:31.584 --> 00:01:34.318
but they are always looking backward in time.

00:01:34.398 --> 00:01:38.946
What is missing is a way to bring that same rigor
up to the present month,

00:01:39.026 --> 00:01:44.700
so that policymakers and scientists can respond to
changes in the ocean sink as they happen.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:51.006
The core contribution is Carbon Monitor Ocean, or
CMO-NRT: a near-real-time, monthly,

00:01:51.086 --> 00:01:56.730
gridded dataset of global surface ocean carbon
dioxide fugacity and air-sea flux,

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:01.181
together with the machine-learning framework that
generates it.

00:02:01.261 --> 00:02:04.643
Rather than replace the trusted community models,

00:02:04.723 --> 00:02:10.720
it updates all ten ocean biogeochemical models and
eight data products from the Global

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.324
Carbon Budget 2022 into a near-real-time framework.

00:02:14.404 --> 00:02:20.330
The dataset and the code are released openly, on
Figshare and on the project website.

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:26.294
The method treats each biogeochemical model or data
product as a target that a neural

00:02:26.374 --> 00:02:30.359
network learns to reproduce from observable
predictors: year,

00:02:30.439 --> 00:02:32.158
month, latitude, longitude,

00:02:32.238 --> 00:02:36.956
and nine environmental variables like sea-surface
temperature, salinity,

00:02:37.036 --> 00:02:38.356
chlorophyll and wind.

00:02:38.436 --> 00:02:46.152
Global grids are cut into small
eighteen-by-eighteen patches and fed through stacked convolutional and linear layers.

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:52.883
The clever part is semi-supervised learning:
alongside a standard supervised error on labeled points,

00:02:52.963 --> 00:02:58.814
the model adds an unsupervised consistency loss
that forces its predictions to agree when

00:02:58.894 --> 00:03:03.278
ten percent versus thirty percent of the input
features are masked.

00:03:03.358 --> 00:03:07.410
Combining the two losses makes the model markedly
more stable.

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:10.556
The resulting dataset delivers monthly,

00:03:10.636 --> 00:03:16.249
gridded maps of global surface-ocean carbon dioxide
fugacity and air-sea flux,

00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:19.168
spanning January 2022 through July 2023,

00:03:19.248 --> 00:03:27.268
built by updating the ten ocean biogeochemical
models and eight data products of the Global Carbon Budget 2022.

00:03:27.348 --> 00:03:33.253
Its inputs draw on satellite and reanalysis
products for chlorophyll, temperature,

00:03:33.333 --> 00:03:38.799
sea ice, mixed-layer depth, salinity, sea-surface
height, pressure and wind,

00:03:38.879 --> 00:03:45.440
and the whole dataset is published openly on
Figshare and refreshed on the project website.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.112
To test the approach,

00:03:47.192 --> 00:03:51.947
the authors held out the most recent two years of
every model and product,

00:03:52.027 --> 00:03:56.455
trained only on earlier data, and then predicted
the withheld period.

00:03:56.535 --> 00:04:02.924
The predictions track the originals remarkably
well: for most of the ten models and eight products,

00:04:03.004 --> 00:04:06.256
the correlation exceeds an R-squared of point nine,

00:04:06.336 --> 00:04:11.091
and even the global monthly aggregates mostly stay
above point eight five.

00:04:11.171 --> 00:04:14.880
On global averages the predictions run just slightly high,

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:18.670
with most differences smaller than three microatmospheres.

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:24.702
Looking model by model reveals a clear pattern: the
biogeochemical models are reproduced

00:04:24.782 --> 00:04:26.851
most faithfully and consistently,

00:04:26.931 --> 00:04:30.434
with several exceeding an R-squared of point nine five,

00:04:30.514 --> 00:04:36.035
while a few data products are noisier and
occasionally scatter away from the fit line.

00:04:36.115 --> 00:04:41.963
Spatial maps of the differences confirm that
agreement is broadly uniform across the globe,

00:04:42.043 --> 00:04:47.890
with the largest residual gaps concentrated in the
Arctic Ocean and the equatorial Pacific.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.491
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:04:50.571 --> 00:04:55.651
The method collapses the roughly one-year reporting
delay down to monthly,

00:04:55.731 --> 00:05:00.532
near-real-time coverage, here spanning January 2022
through July 2023.

00:05:00.612 --> 00:05:07.504
Validation correlations exceed an R-squared of
point nine for most of the eighteen source estimates,

00:05:07.584 --> 00:05:10.712
global aggregates stay above point eight five,

00:05:10.792 --> 00:05:15.663
and global monthly differences are mostly under
three microatmospheres.

00:05:15.743 --> 00:05:22.217
Even the auxiliary near-real-time atmospheric
carbon dioxide model achieves a root-mean-square

00:05:22.297 --> 00:05:24.657
error of just one point seven four,

00:05:24.737 --> 00:05:26.540
about a half-percent error.

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:32.453
The takeaway is simple: by pairing convolutional
networks with semi-supervised learning

00:05:32.533 --> 00:05:35.227
to update trusted models and data products,

00:05:35.307 --> 00:05:42.258
Carbon Monitor Ocean turns a once year-delayed
picture of the global ocean carbon sink into a near-real-time,

00:05:42.338 --> 00:05:43.871
monthly, gridded monitor.

00:05:43.951 --> 00:05:49.484
That gives scientists and policymakers a far
timelier and spatially detailed constraint

00:05:49.564 --> 00:05:52.193
on how much carbon the ocean is taking up,

00:05:52.273 --> 00:05:56.580
right when they need it for the carbon budget and
climate decisions.
